-- title:
--   Increasing bounded continuous approximations preserving convexity or concavity
-- statement:
--   Let $E$ be a real Hausdorff locally convex topological vector space, and let $A\subseteq E$ be convex and von Neumann bounded. Assume the relative topology of $A$ is generated by a countable subfamily of the ambient defining seminorms, and that $A$ is complete and separable in the resulting metrizable additive uniformity. The set $A$ is allowed to be empty. All regularity and convergence below are relative to $A$.
--
--   Write $M(A)$ for the Borel probability measures on $A$ with the weak convergence topology. The barycenter $b(\mu)\in A$ is the Pettis integral of inclusion: every continuous real linear functional $\ell$ is integrable against $\mu$, and $\ell(b(\mu))=\int_A\ell(x)\,\mu(dx)$. The set $A$ is $\mu$-compact when the barycenter preimage of every compact subset of $A$ is compact in $M(A)$; it is pointwise $\mu$-compact when every barycenter fiber is compact.
--
--   Write $C_b(A)$ for the bounded continuous real functions on $A$. For an extended-valued cost $f$, let $\operatorname{co}f$ be its greatest convex minorant and $\overline{\operatorname{co}}f$ its greatest lower semicontinuous convex minorant. A finite lower bound means $f(x)\ge c$ for all $x\in A$ for some $c\in\mathbb R$, so positive infinity is permitted but negative infinity is excluded on $A$.
--
--   Assume $A$ is $\mu$-compact and stable: the midpoint map $A\times A\to A$, $(x,y)\mapsto(x+y)/2$, is open in the relative topologies. Let $f:A\to(-\infty,+\infty]$ be lower semicontinuous with a finite lower bound. If $f$ is convex, there is a sequence $(g_n)$ of bounded continuous real convex functions on $A$ satisfying
--   $$
--   g_n(x)\le g_{n+1}(x)\quad(n\in\mathbb N,\ x\in A),\qquad
--   \lim_{n\to\infty}g_n(x)=f(x)\quad(x\in A).
--   $$
--   If instead $f$ is concave, the same assertion holds with every $g_n$ concave. Extended concavity means convexity of $-f$, and the convention for both signs of infinity at zero weight is $0\cdot(\pm\infty)=0$. The finite bound on each $g_n$ may depend on $n$; the limit can equal positive infinity. No uniform convergence is asserted.
--
--   The result preserves the convex or concave shape during monotone continuous approximation.
-- source:
--   Characterization of convex $μ$-compact sets, arXiv:1004.3792v1, PDF p. 5, Corollary

import Definitions.Def_QAlgorithms_Papers_Independent_Support50_P33

noncomputable section

open Set Filter MeasureTheory TopologicalSpace

open scoped Topology ENNReal

namespace QAlgorithms.Papers.Independent.Support50.P33

open QAlgorithms.Papers.Independent.Frontier18.P10

